```

eth2 is the **r6_r3 backbone link**. Every packet arriving from the backbone (router3/router2/router1/router0 side) and destined for the 172.18.51.x or 172.18.50.x hosts is dropped in the FORWARD chain. The only exception is TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. ip_forward is 1, so the issue is purely the ACL. And router3 can ping router6.eth2 (10.50.1.2) with 0% loss — the link itself is fine.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The nftables/iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically rules applied to traffic arriving on `eth2` (the `r6_r3` backbone link to router3).

**Mechanism:** ip_forward is enabled on router6 (value = 1), so the kernel is willing to route. However, the FORWARD chain contains three DROP rules that fire on all forwarded traffic entering from `eth2`: one drops TCP, one drops UDP, and a final catch-all drops everything else (59 packets/5072 bytes already silenced). Only a narrow exception exists — TCP port 80 from `172.16.10.0/24` destined to `172.18.51.1`. Because ALL inbound-from-backbone traffic is dropped before it reaches the 172.18.51.0/24 or 172.18.50.0/24 segments, no external host can send packets into those subnets. The reverse direction (172.18.51.x hosts sending outbound via router6.eth2) is unaffected — that traffic is FORWARD-chain accepted or uses the OUTPUT/local path — explaining why router6 itself can ping external hosts and pc15 can reach its gateway, yet the path is asymmetrically blocked for transit.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, three DROP rules matching `iifname "eth2"`. The `r6_r3` link (router6.eth2 ↔ router3.eth2) is physically up and carries RIP updates normally.

**Root cause:** An ACL injected into router6's iptables FORWARD chain drops all forwarded traffic arriving from the backbone interface eth2, blackholing the entire 172.18.51.0/24 subnet from the perspective of any source outside that segment.

**Fix:** Delete the three DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain:
`iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP`
`iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP`
`iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP`
(and their nftables equivalents in `table ip filter chain FORWARD`).
=== END ===